{"name":"Wren","hex":"#4a4139","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Wren","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname transferred from the nickname.\nSir Christopher Wren, English architect","Sir Christopher Wren, English architect"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female member of the Royal Navy (originally, a member of the WRNS)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wren"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wren","blocks":[{"text":"Wrens are a family, Troglodytidae, of small brown passerine birds. The family includes 96 species and is divided into 19 genera. All species are restricted to the New World except for the Eurasian wren that is widely distributed in the Old World. In Anglophone regions, the Eurasian wren is commonly known simply as the \"wren\", as it is the originator of the name. The name wren has been applied to other, unrelated birds, particularly the New Zealand wrens (Acanthisittidae) and the Australian wrens (Maluridae)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Troglodytidae_diversity.jpg/330px-Troglodytidae_diversity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wren","wikidata":"Q208304"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Cobb%27s_Wren.png/330px-Cobb%27s_Wren.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Cobb%27s_Wren.png/330px-Cobb%27s_Wren.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cobb's wren is an insular endemic, restricted to the Falkland Islands","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACobb's_Wren.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Campylorhynchus_brunneicapillus_20061226.jpg/500px-Campylorhynchus_brunneicapillus_20061226.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu